  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none mb-12">
    <h2>1. The Inversion Loss Physics: The Triple Conversion Trap</h2>
    <p>
      Solar photovoltaic (PV) panels generate Direct Current (DC) power. Batteries store and release Direct Current (DC) power. However, household appliances run on Alternating Current (AC). The electrical architecture determines how many times power must undergo conversion:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>DC-Coupled Architecture (Single Inversion):</strong> DC power from the rooftop solar array flows directly into an MPPT (Maximum Power Point Tracking) charge controller in the hybrid inverter, charging the battery directly (DC-to-DC) at <strong>98% efficiency</strong>. When the house calls for power, energy converts once (DC-to-AC). Round-trip efficiency is typically <strong>92% to 95%</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>AC-Coupled Architecture (Triple Inversion):</strong> DC solar power converts to AC via rooftop microinverters (DC-to-AC, 3% loss). To charge the battery, power must convert back to DC via the battery's internal charger (AC-to-DC, 4% loss). When the battery discharges into the home, it converts back to AC a third time (DC-to-AC, 4% loss). Cumulative conversion losses consume <strong>12% to 16%</strong> of your harvested solar energy!</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. Direct Comparison Table: AC vs DC Coupling</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
      <table class="w-full text-sm text-left border border-slate-200 rounded-lg">
        <thead class="bg-slate-100 text-slate-800 font-bold border-b border-slate-200">
          <tr>
            <th class="py-3 px-4">Feature / Specification</th>
            <th class="py-3 px-4">DC-Coupled Hybrid</th>
            <th class="py-3 px-4">AC-Coupled Battery</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200 text-slate-700">
          <tr>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-slate-900">Round-Trip Efficiency</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold text-emerald-700">92% - 95% (High)</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 text-amber-700">84% - 88% (Moderate)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
